WEBVTT

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:12.462
Hi, friends. This is the Worry Box.

00:00:12.462 --> 00:00:16.923
Sit tall in your chair. Feet flat on the floor.

00:00:16.923 --> 00:00:19.600
Hands flat on your desk. Shoulders soft.

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:22.277
Take one slow breath with me… and let it go.

00:00:22.277 --> 00:00:33.877
Everyone has worries sometimes. A worry is a thought that asks, "What if…?" — again and again.

00:00:33.877 --> 00:00:40.123
A worry can feel heavy when it stays in your head all day.

00:00:40.123 --> 00:00:46.370
The Worry Box gives a worry a place to rest — so your mind can come back to right now.

00:00:46.370 --> 00:00:55.293
Let's practice with a pretend worry. Here it is: "What if it rains on field day?"

00:00:55.293 --> 00:01:06.893
First, name the worry in words: "I am worried about rain on field day." Naming it makes it one thought — not a whole storm.

00:01:06.893 --> 00:01:14.032
Picture a small box beside you. It is strong, and it has a lid.

00:01:14.032 --> 00:01:20.278
Imagine putting the worry inside the box. Close the lid gently.

00:01:20.278 --> 00:01:27.416
The box will hold it for you. You do not have to carry it right now.

00:01:27.416 --> 00:01:35.447
Come back to right now. Feel your feet on the floor. Hear the sounds in this room.

00:01:35.447 --> 00:01:42.586
Take one slow breath… and let it go. This moment is where you are.

00:01:42.586 --> 00:01:51.509
If the worry pops back into your head, that is okay. Worries do that. Picture the box again, and let the worry rest inside. Then come back to now.

00:01:51.509 --> 00:01:55.971
And if a worry ever feels too big for the box, tell your teacher. Teachers are helpers.

00:01:55.971 --> 00:01:58.648
Your worry is resting in the box. Your mind is here, in this room, right now.

00:01:58.648 --> 00:02:02.217
Hands down. One more slow breath… and let it go.

00:02:02.217 --> 00:02:04.001
Eyes open. Look around your classroom.

00:02:04.001 --> 00:02:05.786
You're ready for what's next.
